---
import { MOS_VERSION } from '../lib/version'
import UpdateFlow from './UpdateFlow.astro'
---

<section class="band">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <span class="mos-eyebrow">Questions</span>
      <h2 class="mos-section-title">Honest answers</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details open>
        <summary>Do I need to be technical? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p><strong style="color:var(--mos-color-text-strong)">Short answer:</strong> a little, for the install. Home servers are notoriously fiddly and MOS removes most of that, but you still have to get it onto a machine. On a cloud server that means following a six-minute video and pasting one command. On your own hardware it means writing the image to a USB stick with a free tool such as <a href="https://rufus.ie/">Rufus</a> or <a href="https://etcher.balena.io/">balenaEtcher</a> and booting a spare machine from it. If you can follow a video guide with a little patience, you can do this.</p>
        <p>Once you're in, the hard part is over. Everything happens in the browser, and installing an app is pick, Install, open: the settings are chosen for you, the address and the padlock are arranged, and the next backup covers it. <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/getting-started/">How to get started</a> lays out both paths side by side.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is it really free — what does it actually cost? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>MOS and every app in the catalog are free and open source: no license keys, no subscriptions, no paid tier.</p>
        <p>The only cost is the machine it runs on: a little electricity at home, or about $20–25 a month for a rented cloud server. A managed option, where you pay MOS to run it all for you, is coming for people who'd rather not touch a server at all.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where does my data actually live? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>On the machine you installed MOS on. Nothing passes through a MOS-owned cloud, the platform has no telemetry, and there is no sign-up with us: we don't know that you installed it.</p>
        <p>The one exception is this page, which counts anonymous visits with analytics we host ourselves. Your suite has none. The <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/privacy/">privacy policy</a> lists every connection your server makes.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do my apps stay up to date? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <UpdateFlow />
        <p>Not on their own: nothing changes on your server until you press the button. Behind the scenes we watch each catalog app's releases. A new version gets its package rebuilt, the releases in between read for breaking changes, its privacy assessment redone, and the result tested on a real server: a fresh install, an update from the version you'd be coming from, and a backup-and-restore drill, with every outside connection the app makes recorded. Only then is it signed into the catalog, and a version that breaks something is held back instead.</p>
        <p>Your server checks that signed catalog every few hours and shows an <strong style="color:var(--mos-color-text-strong)">Update available</strong> badge, so a fix for your password manager never waits for a MOS release. You see what changes first, including whether the privacy grade moved, then you press the button. MOS builds the new version before stopping the old one, and if the update fails it puts the app back or tells you exactly where it stopped. <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/guides/updates/">How updates work</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I install an app that isn't in the catalog? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. Any GitHub repository with a small <code>.mos</code> folder is an app source: paste its URL into the Apps search and MOS shows what it publishes, and what each app asks for, before anything is built. One repository can carry one app or a whole catalog, so a collection someone curates is added once and browsed like the rest.</p>
        <p>These apps are labelled <strong style="color:var(--mos-color-text-strong)">External · Unverified</strong> everywhere, because we haven't reviewed them and won't pretend otherwise, and MOS runs them with fewer permissions than catalog apps. Building one runs the publisher's own instructions on your server, so it's your call; MOS makes sure it's an informed one.</p>
        <p>No package yet? It is a manifest plus a Dockerfile, with a complete example repository to copy, or one prompt with the MOS plugin for Claude Code: <code>/mos:add-app</code> writes the package for you. <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/guides/apps/#you-found-an-app-on-github-now-what">Bringing your own apps</a> walks through it.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What if I want to leave? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>Everything runs on standard open-source apps with standard data formats, and your backups are plain <a class="mos-link" href="https://restic.net/" rel="noopener">restic</a> repositories, encrypted with your recovery key before they leave the server.</p>
        <p>If MOS vanished tomorrow, that key and the restic command-line tool would still open every backup you have, on any machine. The way out does not run through us.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>It's beta — is it safe to use? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>MOS is version {MOS_VERSION} and improving quickly. Every change runs an automated test suite before it lands, the whole install-to-apps flow is driven on real machines before a release, and every image is pinned to an exact digest. The changelog is public.</p>
        <p>Trust is the product, so here's the honest part: pre-1.0 releases can include breaking changes. Treat MOS as ready for evaluation and non-critical use for now: keep an independent copy of anything you can't afford to lose, and take a backup before you update. The button is right there.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
